The NAO page of the top-deaths[*] is a bit of a chaos; there are a lot
specialisations that seem irrelevant and could be condensed significantly.

The whole list currently contains 17000+ entries, and I tried to apply
some substitutions that reduced the number of entries to now less than
1300 entries.

For example: deaths from soldier ants alone have 311(!) entries, while my
condensation results in just 5 entries for soldier ants:

  28372 killed by a soldier ant
    344 killed by a hallucinogen-distorted soldier ant
    332 poisoned by a soldier ant
     67 killed by an invisible soldier ant
      1 killed by a soldier ant priest(ess) of some god


The condensing substitutions that I applied are (somewhat formalized)...

+  ignore the substring "(with the Amulet)"

+  "killed by .*, while (.*)"  -->   "killed by something, while \1"
+  "killed by .*, the shopkeeper"  -->  "killed by a shopkeeper"
+  "slipped while mounting a saddled .*"  -->
        "slipped while mounting a saddled creature"
+  " elemental of .*"  -->  " elemental of some god"
+  "killed by a worthless piece of .* glass"  -->
        "killed by a worthless piece of glass"
+  "poisoned by a rotted .* corpse"  -->
        "poisoned by a rotted creature corpse"
+  "killed by the wrath of .*"  -->  "killed by the wrath of some god"
+  "killed by touching .*"  -->  "killed by touching an artifact"
+  "(killed by a.* of) [A-Z].*"  ->  "\1 some god"

+  ignore the pattern " called.*"
+  ignore the pattern " named.*"

+  " ghost of.*/," ghost of someone"
+  "choked on.*/,"choked on something"
+  " by kicking.*/," by kicking something"
+  " priest(ess)? of[^,]*$"  -->  " priest(ess) of some god"
+  " priest(ess)? of[^,]*,"  -->  " priest(ess) of some god,"


I want do explain some of the decisions behind the substitutions...

If you are helpless, or frozen, etc., or if you consume a rotten corpse,
or choke on it, the creature/corpse that kills you is of minor interest.
The slipping is more important than what creature you tried to mount.
Often specific gods are named in death reasons, but it's not important.
The names of monsters or objects are ignored. etc.

I have not touched the attributes 'invisible', 'hallu-distorted, etc.,
because I think those carry a double relevance; what kills you and in
what phasical state you have brought yourself (or have been brought by
something/someone).


